00:15:50.072 --> 00:15:56.170
Or did a little exploration, I'll be
coming to that side of the equation next,

00:15:56.170 --> 00:16:00.740
so maybe Shawn can get us
kicked us off from Sonia For

00:16:00.740 --> 00:16:04.640
those going back to the same job,
industry, employer, what was the biggest

00:16:04.640 --> 00:16:07.710
takeaway from Stanford GSB
since it wasn't a career pivot?

00:16:09.310 --> 00:16:13.027
&gt;&gt; Yeah, that's a great question and
actually when I was at Michigan undergrad,

00:16:13.027 --> 00:16:16.036
I was actually in the undergraduate
business school there too.

00:16:16.036 --> 00:16:19.372
So [LAUGH] folks will really
ask me why am I going back and

00:16:19.372 --> 00:16:23.650
getting an MBA, and the way I thought
about it was sort of twofold.

00:16:23.650 --> 00:16:26.462
So going to business school versus not.

00:16:26.462 --> 00:16:30.320
And then, why Stanford Business School
versus any other business school?

00:16:30.320 --> 00:16:34.089
So on the first part of that question,
I think the biggest thing for

00:16:34.089 --> 00:16:37.336
me was network,
not only within the folks in your class.

00:16:37.336 --> 00:16:41.884
But also the full alumni network
that you get access to and

00:16:41.884 --> 00:16:46.149
really becomes incredibly
supportive from day one,

00:16:46.149 --> 00:16:51.330
that kind of GSB offers,
I think that's probably number one.

00:16:51.330 --> 00:16:56.272
Number two was for my peers who were also
in private equity or going back into

00:16:56.272 --> 00:17:00.837
the industry or the sector that I
also worked in and was going back to.

00:17:00.837 --> 00:17:04.008
You kind of see how their
firms looked at the world and

00:17:04.008 --> 00:17:08.655
thought about investments and
you pick their brain, they pick yours, and

00:17:08.655 --> 00:17:12.736
it's kind of a good learning
experience from that perspective.

00:17:12.736 --> 00:17:16.501
And that you can see the landscape
a little bit better than when you're just

00:17:16.501 --> 00:17:19.391
within your own firm or
your own group in private equity.

00:17:19.391 --> 00:17:21.490
And then the third thing, which kind of,

00:17:21.490 --> 00:17:24.827
to the good segue into Stamford
versus other business schools,

00:17:24.827 --> 00:17:28.684
is the curriculum outside of what I
would call the core business classes.

00:17:28.684 --> 00:17:32.757
So I wasn't going to Standford or
getting an MBA to learn accounting or

00:17:32.757 --> 00:17:34.362
take a marketing course.

00:17:34.362 --> 00:17:37.982
It was more about the soft skills and I
think that's where Stanford really shines.

00:17:37.982 --> 00:17:40.566
I think a lot of folks go
to business school and

00:17:40.566 --> 00:17:42.732
they want to learn how to become a CEO.

00:17:42.732 --> 00:17:44.883
I was more interested in,
what do you need to do and

00:17:44.883 --> 00:17:46.792
what do you need to learn
to become a leader?

00:17:46.792 --> 00:17:49.173
And I think that's where
Stanford really shines.

00:17:49.173 --> 00:17:51.591
And we've walked through some
of those classes already and

00:17:51.591 --> 00:17:53.480
I'm happy to highlight a few more.

00:17:53.480 --> 00:17:57.675
And then the last thing for me
specifically is I invest specifically in

00:17:57.675 --> 00:18:01.444
technology and Stanford being
right in Palo Alto and so close to

00:18:01.444 --> 00:18:06.090
San Francisco and San Jose, you're kind
of in the epicenter of technology.

00:18:06.090 --> 00:18:10.985
So the way that, I guess, reflects
itself is one, a lot of speakers who

00:18:10.985 --> 00:18:15.653
are in the area, this is pre-COVID,
would come to the classroom.

00:18:15.653 --> 00:18:20.966
And you can build those relationships
just within those 45-minute sessions or

00:18:20.966 --> 00:18:24.040
however long, after class and follow ups.

00:18:24.040 --> 00:18:26.729
And the second is there's
a bunch of career tracks and

00:18:26.729 --> 00:18:29.734
kind of club tracks where you'll
just go explore the area.

00:18:29.734 --> 00:18:34.565
So, kind of being in this community was a
big pull for me for Stanford specifically.

00:18:34.565 --> 00:18:36.957
So I hope that answered the question.

00:18:36.957 --> 00:18:37.816
&gt;&gt; Yeah, it did, thank you.

00:18:37.816 --> 00:18:42.270
And we'll actually come back to some
of those other leadership development

00:18:42.270 --> 00:18:46.953
courses and lessons from there, because I
see a number of questions in the Q&amp;A box.

00:18:46.953 --> 00:18:49.946
So, hot topics, so
we'll definitely get there.

00:18:49.946 --> 00:18:55.030
For our other panelists, whether it
was a small pivot or a larger pivot.

00:18:55.030 --> 00:18:57.862
Can you talk about how Stanford
helped you along that journey,

00:18:57.862 --> 00:19:00.680
even if it was just a little
bit of exploration?

00:19:00.680 --> 00:19:04.090
Can anybody shed a little
bit of light there?

00:19:05.820 --> 00:19:07.113
&gt;&gt; Sure, I can jump in.

00:19:07.113 --> 00:19:11.942
So I actually was 99.9% sure
I was going to go back into

00:19:11.942 --> 00:19:15.780
venture capital or at least a startup.

00:19:15.780 --> 00:19:19.807
I'd been living in the Bay Area and
I loved the whole ecosystem.

00:19:19.807 --> 00:19:24.383
And so when I got to Stanford,
I just wanted to experiment and

00:19:24.383 --> 00:19:26.420
try different things.

00:19:26.420 --> 00:19:30.789
And so it was one of these experiments
that actually led me to discover this

00:19:30.789 --> 00:19:36.120
other area within financial services but
completely different type of work.

00:19:36.120 --> 00:19:39.779
And it was kind of like one
coffee chat led to one interview,

00:19:39.779 --> 00:19:44.707
led to one internship, and then before
I knew it, I'd kind of walked down this

00:19:44.707 --> 00:19:49.660
other path and found something that
was much better suited for me.

00:19:49.660 --> 00:19:53.896
So I would say,
even if you are 99.9% sure of something,

00:19:53.896 --> 00:19:59.518
just opening yourself up to exploration
is really wonderful and gives you chances

00:19:59.518 --> 00:20:05.400
to just learn things about yourself
even if you don't change career paths.

00:20:05.400 --> 00:20:09.891
But I would say the other thing as well is
you just encounter so many perspectives.

00:20:09.891 --> 00:20:12.906
You encounter people from so
many different backgrounds,

00:20:12.906 --> 00:20:17.115
whether that's places they grew up,
industries that they worked with.

00:20:17.115 --> 00:20:21.486
And so I would say, like Shawn alluded to
this, that even if you're going back into

00:20:21.486 --> 00:20:25.544
a similar line of work or a similar job,
you will learn so much about the rest of

00:20:25.544 --> 00:20:29.375
the world and other businesses that
will only make you more successful.

00:20:31.255 --> 00:20:36.130
&gt;&gt; Cong, I have a question, you mentioned
from one conversation to an interview,

00:20:36.130 --> 00:20:41.080
were these classmates, were these alumni,
who were some of these folks that kind

00:20:41.080 --> 00:20:44.180
of serendipitously [LAUGH]
led you on this journey?

00:20:45.522 --> 00:20:48.130
&gt;&gt; It was a path with many
different mentors but

00:20:48.130 --> 00:20:53.006
I would say it started with classmates,
just hey, what did you do before school?

00:20:53.006 --> 00:20:54.027
Why did you like that?

00:20:54.027 --> 00:20:55.896
Why didn't you like that?

00:20:55.896 --> 00:20:58.777
This company is coming to campus
because we have a lot of alums, and

00:20:58.777 --> 00:21:01.710
they're going to come and
talk about their line of work.

00:21:01.710 --> 00:21:07.461
Great, they have a formal internship
program, let's assign you a mentor.

00:21:07.461 --> 00:21:10.060
What does it take to prepare for
these interviews?

00:21:10.060 --> 00:21:12.272
And it kind of just,
every step of the way,

00:21:12.272 --> 00:21:16.550
I don't think it's something I could
have done without the ecosystem.

00:21:16.550 --> 00:21:21.298
And that ecosystem is more about
the people, present students and

00:21:21.298 --> 00:21:25.469
alumni that kind of come
together to form that ecosystem.

00:21:25.469 --> 00:21:26.790
&gt;&gt; Nice, thank you.

00:21:26.790 --> 00:21:28.105
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:21:28.105 --> 00:21:31.481
&gt;&gt; And then Brian,
there's a couple questions here,

00:21:31.481 --> 00:21:34.406
and I might extend this
after your response.

00:21:34.406 --> 00:21:38.567
Did you know you wanted to
go into entrepreneurship or

00:21:38.567 --> 00:21:43.600
that you wanted to start a company,
how did that happen?

00:21:43.600 --> 00:21:47.773
And then kind of at a broader level for
the other folks,

00:21:47.773 --> 00:21:52.990
how solid of an idea did you have
coming into business school?

00:21:52.990 --> 00:21:57.746
There's a question here from Caroline
about how helpful the GSB was at

00:21:57.746 --> 00:22:02.195
helping you identify your passion or
the things you wanted to do.

00:22:02.195 --> 00:22:05.331
Ans so, I think we might have
alluded to a little bit there but

00:22:05.331 --> 00:22:07.310
maybe let's start with you, Brian.

00:22:07.310 --> 00:22:09.380
Did you know you want it to go into this?

00:22:09.380 --> 00:22:12.738
And then we can blow up the conversation.

00:22:12.738 --> 00:22:17.507
&gt;&gt; Yeah, kind of,
I started business school and

00:22:17.507 --> 00:22:21.923
the goal in my heart
was to start a company.

00:22:21.923 --> 00:22:26.280
One of the first classes I had in
the fall quarter of my first year,

00:22:26.280 --> 00:22:31.670
the teacher had us write down in class
what our dream goal essentially was.

00:22:31.670 --> 00:22:36.643
And that was my goal, so early on, my goal
was to start a company, but it was also

00:22:36.643 --> 00:22:42.200
something that I was very afraid of and
I didn't know if I had what it took.

00:22:42.200 --> 00:22:45.979
I didn't have a specific idea,
I didn't have a partner, I mean,

00:22:45.979 --> 00:22:50.297
there were a lot of things that I thought
I didn't have that would keep me from

00:22:50.297 --> 00:22:53.910
achieving that goal, so
it felt very scary to me.

00:22:53.910 --> 00:22:58.515
So, part of business school for
me was really through a lot of ways

00:22:58.515 --> 00:23:02.728
building the confidence that
it's not such a scary thing.

00:23:02.728 --> 00:23:05.648
And then you can de-risk and de-risk and

00:23:05.648 --> 00:23:10.405
de-risk by making various decisions,
like who you partner with,

00:23:10.405 --> 00:23:14.509
and what you work on, and
this is stuff you can start to do.

00:23:14.509 --> 00:23:18.144
And that was really, I think,
a two-year process for me.

00:23:18.144 --> 00:23:22.140
But I've reached the ultimate point
where a few months before graduation,

00:23:22.140 --> 00:23:25.575
my partner and I were mentally
prepared to commit to each other and

00:23:25.575 --> 00:23:27.948
to the rest of the people
we are working with,

00:23:27.948 --> 00:23:32.220
that this is something that we are really
going to dedicate ourselves to.

00:23:32.220 --> 00:23:36.290
And who knows if it works out but
we're going to give it everything we can.

00:23:36.290 --> 00:23:40.953
And I think Stanford absolutely was a huge
part for me of building that confidence

00:23:40.953 --> 00:23:45.348
and gaining some tools along the way
of how to go about starting a company.

00:23:45.348 --> 00:23:49.117
&gt;&gt; Nice, and then Khalil,
as you were at the GSB,

00:23:49.117 --> 00:23:54.877
did you have a clear thread of what you
want to do or was there exploration?

00:23:54.877 --> 00:23:58.194
And how did you go about identifying
those things that were meaningful for

00:23:58.194 --> 00:23:59.380
you in the career search?

00:24:00.840 --> 00:24:06.417
Yeah, I think I knew things I enjoy
I mean broadly things that I enjoy,

00:24:06.417 --> 00:24:08.888
so I knew I enjoyed marketing.

00:24:08.888 --> 00:24:13.693
I was pretty sure I wanted
to be an operator and

00:24:13.693 --> 00:24:17.114
play less of a consultant role.

00:24:17.114 --> 00:24:21.870
But outside of that I wasn't really and
I knew I was interested in technology.

00:24:21.870 --> 00:24:26.823
So that's like one of the main
reasons why I chose GSB is because I

00:24:26.823 --> 00:24:30.240
knew I'd be surrounded in Silicon Valley.

00:24:30.240 --> 00:24:34.669
But I think being at the GSB
really opened my eyes to all of

00:24:34.669 --> 00:24:38.730
the different opportunities
that were out there.

00:24:38.730 --> 00:24:42.212
And just with classmates, right,
even just on this call, there's so

00:24:42.212 --> 00:24:44.664
many people with so
many different backgrounds.

00:24:44.664 --> 00:24:49.259
So like I started off the GSB with just
talking to a lot of people about what they

00:24:49.259 --> 00:24:51.099
did before business school and

00:24:51.099 --> 00:24:54.440
trying to figure out if that
would be interesting to me.

00:24:54.440 --> 00:24:58.199
Even venture capital,
I was like, that seems cool,

00:24:58.199 --> 00:25:03.664
there's some marketing strategy there,
there's some business strategy there.

00:25:03.664 --> 00:25:07.192
But talking to someone I was like,
I don't know if that's,

00:25:07.192 --> 00:25:11.133
when I really dug into it,
it really helped me figure out that okay,

00:25:11.133 --> 00:25:15.050
no that wasn't the perfect spot for
me at this moment.

00:25:15.050 --> 00:25:19.640
So, I think it really just being immersed
by so many people as a lot of people on

00:25:19.640 --> 00:25:22.905
this call mentioned,
was really, really valuable.

00:25:22.905 --> 00:25:27.467
And yeah and
helped solidify that product marketing was

00:25:27.467 --> 00:25:30.289
a role that I wanted to do post GSB.

00:25:31.320 --> 00:25:34.192
&gt;&gt; Nice, I know there's a report
that comes out every year from our

00:25:34.192 --> 00:25:36.330
Career Management Center
about the outcomes.

00:25:36.330 --> 00:25:40.773
And in that report, they talk a little bit
about where these opportunities came from.

00:25:40.773 --> 00:25:45.280
I think the split was a third
come from opportunities through

00:25:45.280 --> 00:25:50.595
the Career Management Center,
a third come from students and alumni.

00:25:50.595 --> 00:25:55.477
And another third come from, that might be
self source or if they were sponsored and

00:25:55.477 --> 00:25:58.650
are returning to their company from there.

00:25:58.650 --> 00:26:03.812
I'm curious, has anybody on this call
worked with the Career Management Center,

00:26:03.812 --> 00:26:08.324
or were you representative of the other
two thirds of the outcomes here?

00:26:11.256 --> 00:26:14.400
&gt;&gt; I definitely worked with
the Career Management Center.

00:26:14.400 --> 00:26:18.790
&gt;&gt; Can you talk a little bit about how
they supported you through that or

00:26:18.790 --> 00:26:20.494
some insight into there?

00:26:20.494 --> 00:26:25.864
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so
I worked pretty heavily with Grace and

00:26:25.864 --> 00:26:32.316
Gino who are just two of
the faculty in the Career Center.

00:26:32.316 --> 00:26:36.438
They're both really great, they're both
kind of specialize in different things.

00:26:36.438 --> 00:26:43.147
So, I spent a ton of time with Gino on
actual preparing for actual interviews.

00:26:43.147 --> 00:26:48.441
So, we would go through,
I would set a time, we'd have 30 minutes

00:26:48.441 --> 00:26:54.375
to just talk through the cases and then
I use Grace as my mentor of long-term,

00:26:54.375 --> 00:26:59.889
what should I be doing in my life and
how should I be thinking about that?

00:26:59.889 --> 00:27:04.235
They've both been super invaluable,
and yeah, so

00:27:04.235 --> 00:27:09.670
they've actually both of them
have been instrumental in helping

00:27:09.670 --> 00:27:15.824
me determine what role and what types
of companies I would want to work for.

00:27:15.824 --> 00:27:17.919
&gt;&gt; Right, and Brian I saw you smiling and

00:27:17.919 --> 00:27:21.982
nodding as [LAUGH] Khalil is talking
about some of the folks he worked with,

00:27:21.982 --> 00:27:25.799
were you able to interact with
anybody from the CMC during your time?

00:27:25.799 --> 00:27:29.351
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I was smiling because I
worked with Grace a lot too, and

00:27:29.351 --> 00:27:31.210
I would totally recommend her.

00:27:31.210 --> 00:27:33.288
She's fantastic.

00:27:33.288 --> 00:27:36.764
Yeah, I worked with them
less on a full time job, but

00:27:36.764 --> 00:27:41.189
part of my theory of testing
entrepreneurship was to see how I liked

00:27:41.189 --> 00:27:47.370
working in a non-financial services role,
which is what I was previously doing.

00:27:47.370 --> 00:27:50.291
And so, I used my summer,
between the first and

00:27:50.291 --> 00:27:55.160
second year to do two internships that
were totally different from finance.

00:27:55.160 --> 00:27:58.748
One was at a healthcare company
doing product work, and

00:27:58.748 --> 00:28:02.196
one was at an ecommerce
company doing strategy work.

00:28:02.196 --> 00:28:06.869
And those were just tests for me on how I
liked those types of different roles, and

00:28:06.869 --> 00:28:10.994
Grace was invaluable towards thinking
about how I should pick those and

00:28:10.994 --> 00:28:14.450
figuring out companies that
will be good fits within that.

00:28:14.450 --> 00:28:18.024
So, they were very helpful for me.

00:28:18.024 --> 00:28:19.171
&gt;&gt; Career Management Center,

00:28:19.171 --> 00:28:21.830
awesome people,
I get to work on a floor with them.

00:28:21.830 --> 00:28:24.137
It's amazing anytime I get
to interact with them and

00:28:24.137 --> 00:28:26.610
talk about kind of how they
help support our community.

00:28:27.780 --> 00:28:32.841
So, we're going to shift gears a little
bit, there's a question here from Nicole,

00:28:32.841 --> 00:28:37.829
about, if after graduation, if you could
go back in time and start the program all

00:28:37.829 --> 00:28:43.050
over again, what advice would you give
yourself as an incoming MBA candidate?

00:28:43.050 --> 00:28:46.293
Usually I'll save a question like this for
the end, but

00:28:46.293 --> 00:28:49.612
I have a different end question
of kind of to motivate us.

00:28:49.612 --> 00:28:53.116
But as you think about yourself,
two, three years ago,

00:28:53.116 --> 00:28:57.211
what advice would you give yourself
as an incoming MBA candidate?

00:28:57.211 --> 00:29:00.211
And open anybody wants to start.

00:29:12.343 --> 00:29:16.636
&gt;&gt; I can start, I actually got this
advice from several people but

00:29:16.636 --> 00:29:21.019
it's kind of hard to contextualize
it until you're at campus.

00:29:21.019 --> 00:29:26.227
But a lot really happens,
in your first quarter, just school,

00:29:26.227 --> 00:29:30.675
social, sometimes even
the internship recruiting.

00:29:30.675 --> 00:29:33.920
And I think the biggest thing
that I wish I knew was,

00:29:33.920 --> 00:29:35.967
the ability to say no to things.

00:29:35.967 --> 00:29:37.889
And kind of defensive calendaring,

00:29:37.889 --> 00:29:42.052
and because I think folks will just fill
up your Google calendar with stuff and

00:29:42.052 --> 00:29:45.337
you want to go to everything
truthfully because it's a lot of fun.

00:29:45.337 --> 00:29:48.747
It can get a little overwhelming at times,

00:29:48.747 --> 00:29:52.734
I consider myself like
a fairly outgoing person.

00:29:52.734 --> 00:29:54.318
But when you meet everyone at Stanford,

00:29:54.318 --> 00:29:57.361
everyone's dialed like 12 out of 10
particularly in the first quarter.

00:29:57.361 --> 00:29:59.612
So it's fun but
it feels a little crazy and

00:29:59.612 --> 00:30:02.508
that first quarter you're
really asking yourself,

00:30:02.508 --> 00:30:06.254
well how did I get in here because
you're meeting so many amazing people.

00:30:06.254 --> 00:30:10.264
That alone time actually I think really,
really helps.

00:30:10.264 --> 00:30:14.420
My girlfriend at the time, now fiancé,
she lives in San Francisco and

00:30:14.420 --> 00:30:18.929
one of my favorite parts truthfully of
the week was whenever we go visit her,

00:30:18.929 --> 00:30:22.109
because it's an hour long
car ride to San Francisco.

00:30:22.109 --> 00:30:27.089
And it was a one-time where you're not
kind of surrounded by people talking.

00:30:27.089 --> 00:30:31.330
And I know a lot of folks got
that through, through exercise or

00:30:31.330 --> 00:30:35.491
meditation or other ways, but
I would highly recommend it,

00:30:35.491 --> 00:30:40.402
block off time for yourself because,
if you don't it no one else will.

00:30:40.402 --> 00:30:44.945
&gt;&gt; I love the phrase defensive calendaring
I think I'm going to use that all

00:30:44.945 --> 00:30:45.869
the time now.

00:30:45.869 --> 00:30:50.050
Any your tips for how people might be
able to defensively calendar especially

00:30:50.050 --> 00:30:51.990
knowing like the GSB environment?

00:30:53.780 --> 00:30:57.530
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I would just block off like
30 minutes to an hour every day.

00:30:58.910 --> 00:31:03.808
Whether it's a long stretch of class or
a bunch of back to back coffee chats

00:31:03.808 --> 00:31:07.523
where you are kind of all quote unquote,
on with folks.

00:31:07.523 --> 00:31:13.349
That's usually when I would do it
from the gym or read or podcast.

00:31:13.349 --> 00:31:14.857
I think it's plugging that in.

00:31:14.857 --> 00:31:18.547
And a lot of this settles too after the
first quarter, once everyone kind of gets

00:31:18.547 --> 00:31:22.075
a little bit more comfortable with
the environment and themselves and their

00:31:22.075 --> 00:31:26.068
classmates, but it's that first quarter
work, it can feel a little overwhelming.

00:31:26.068 --> 00:31:29.916
&gt;&gt; So in addition to defensive
calendaring, what other advice might

00:31:29.916 --> 00:31:34.050
one of the other panelists give
yourself as an incoming MBA candidate?

00:31:35.710 --> 00:31:40.686
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I think a lot of what Shawn
says definitely resonates with me.

00:31:40.686 --> 00:31:45.775
I, on that note of it being super busy,
you're going

00:31:45.775 --> 00:31:50.864
to start day one and
it's going to be crazy busy with so

00:31:50.864 --> 00:31:56.767
many different things and
activities you can focus on.

00:31:56.767 --> 00:32:00.126
And I've never been busier in
my entire life, there was not,

00:32:00.126 --> 00:32:02.920
there's, You don't have
time to do everything.

00:32:02.920 --> 00:32:05.550
So you're going to have to prioritize.

00:32:05.550 --> 00:32:09.416
And I think having a clear
mindset of before,

00:32:09.416 --> 00:32:13.195
of what you want to get
out of the experience.

00:32:13.195 --> 00:32:16.995
What you want to get out of each,
whether you want to do it each year,

00:32:16.995 --> 00:32:19.612
each quarter,
however you want to look at it.

00:32:19.612 --> 00:32:23.860
But like coming in with that and
making sure that how you split your time

00:32:23.860 --> 00:32:30.010
represents what your priorities are, is,
in my perspective, incredibly important.

00:32:30.010 --> 00:32:31.560
So, that'd be my advice.

00:32:31.560 --> 00:32:34.681
It's like figure out exactly what
you want to get out there and

00:32:34.681 --> 00:32:38.296
make sure that your time, your energy
is devoted in those categories.

00:32:38.296 --> 00:32:44.880
Because it's like so easy to get wrapped
up in everything else that's going on.

00:32:44.880 --> 00:32:49.231
Everything that's thrown at you
like social events, as I mentioned,

00:32:49.231 --> 00:32:51.270
there's going to be so much in q1.

00:32:51.270 --> 00:32:54.017
But then all of a sudden,
the first recruiting is going to be

00:32:54.017 --> 00:32:57.565
consultant recruiting is going to like
up and then you're going to be like,

00:32:57.565 --> 00:32:58.844
should I be a consultant.

00:32:58.844 --> 00:33:02.832
And you're going to end, it's like you
didn't want to be a consultant before but

00:33:02.832 --> 00:33:05.855
everything gets, it's so
easy to get wrapped up in mixer,

00:33:05.855 --> 00:33:09.510
like really having a clear
vision of of what you want.

00:33:09.510 --> 00:33:13.546
&gt;&gt; It's funny when everybody on the call
kind of laughs at something because

00:33:13.546 --> 00:33:17.255
that feeling just kind of kicks
back in as you were talking about,

00:33:17.255 --> 00:33:20.662
consulting recruiting starts,
do I want to be a consultant.

00:33:20.662 --> 00:33:23.660
I just saw a lot of faces light up,

00:33:23.660 --> 00:33:27.847
because fairly that thought
process very common.

00:33:27.847 --> 00:33:33.158
Let's switch gears again, I did promise to
talk about the leadership curriculum and

00:33:33.158 --> 00:33:37.009
that's one of the things that we
hear a lot about on our panel.

00:33:37.009 --> 00:33:41.893
So, door says question at the top about
what area of your leadership potential

00:33:41.893 --> 00:33:45.746
did you feel have the greatest
opportunity for development?

00:33:45.746 --> 00:33:49.710
And how did GSB help fill those
gaps slash strengthen those areas?

00:33:49.710 --> 00:33:54.022
So I'm going to broaden this question
a little bit because I think coming

00:33:54.022 --> 00:33:57.748
into business school,
you might have thought about, hey,

00:33:57.748 --> 00:34:00.540
here's an area that I
would like to develop.

00:34:01.540 --> 00:34:06.249
But coming out of the experience,
sometimes there's areas that you didn't

00:34:06.249 --> 00:34:11.340
realize, were opportunities for growth and
those were aha moments for you.

00:34:11.340 --> 00:34:13.760
So we'll open it up.

00:34:13.760 --> 00:34:19.502
So either areas that you knew you wanted
to develop and how did you kind of target

00:34:19.502 --> 00:34:24.913
that or, hey, realization of I did
develop greatly in this area and how.

00:34:24.913 --> 00:34:28.884
So either looking forward or looking back.

00:34:28.884 --> 00:34:33.811
Maybe Brian because you have one of
the fresher [LAUGH] experiences.

00:34:33.811 --> 00:34:38.344
Can you get us kicked off and
then Sang, we'll come to you next.

00:34:38.344 --> 00:34:43.636
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so my answer,
when I started at Stanford when I applied,

00:34:43.636 --> 00:34:47.655
I was not,
I was focused on entrepreneurship and

00:34:47.655 --> 00:34:51.980
I think exploring
the world beyond finance.

00:34:51.980 --> 00:34:58.670
But I wasn't focused on
the Arbuckle Leadership Program.

00:34:58.670 --> 00:35:01.581
But that was something I
ended up focusing on so,

00:35:01.581 --> 00:35:05.418
what that is it's a program that
you apply to in your first year.

00:35:05.418 --> 00:35:09.627
And it's focused on communication
with other people and

00:35:09.627 --> 00:35:14.160
how groups work together and
how teams work together.

00:35:14.160 --> 00:35:18.557
And it's also I think, focused on knowing
yourself and there's classes that

00:35:18.557 --> 00:35:22.954
are directed towards, you're just getting
a finer point on what you value and

00:35:22.954 --> 00:35:27.490
why you value it and how you want to
manifest those things in your life.

00:35:27.490 --> 00:35:30.325
And so as I started taking those classes,

00:35:30.325 --> 00:35:33.330
I think I saw that I was
taking a lot out of it.

00:35:33.330 --> 00:35:38.194
And that it was really a period
of a lot of personal growth for

00:35:38.194 --> 00:35:43.345
me in terms of just really being,
for the first time of my life,

00:35:43.345 --> 00:35:48.015
I think rigorous about what I
was searching for and why and

00:35:48.015 --> 00:35:50.955
how far I was willing to go to get it.

00:35:50.955 --> 00:35:55.738
And how I could really communicate better,
because I think I was actually fairly

00:35:55.738 --> 00:35:59.340
closed off in my communication
before Business School.

00:36:01.140 --> 00:36:06.128
So yeah, I think for me, reflecting
back on the experience it has to

00:36:06.128 --> 00:36:09.989
be my communication,
was the biggest growth area.

00:36:09.989 --> 00:36:14.412
And from that I think it's, part of it's
about knowing what you want and part of

00:36:14.412 --> 00:36:18.970
it's about knowing how to say things and
how to communicate your needs and desires

00:36:18.970 --> 00:36:23.423
to other people in a way that's also
respectful of their own needs and desires.

00:36:23.423 --> 00:36:27.035
And there's whole classes
on that at Stanford, but

00:36:27.035 --> 00:36:32.467
that ended up being by far my, I think,
most important takeaway from school.

00:36:32.467 --> 00:36:37.228
&gt;&gt; You use this really fascinating
word rigorous, like the rigorous

00:36:37.228 --> 00:36:42.170
kind of approach to some of this,
personal core skill development.

00:36:43.850 --> 00:36:48.720
What were some of the structures that
you would say contributed to that rigor?

00:36:48.720 --> 00:36:51.811
Like how would you kind
of put words to that.

00:36:51.811 --> 00:36:56.683
&gt;&gt; Well, I think something like Coolio
was just saying is part of I think

00:36:56.683 --> 00:37:01.830
was the Business School gets you
to do is to reflect all the time.

00:37:01.830 --> 00:37:07.160
And so you go and you take some action,
and then you think about how did that go?

00:37:07.160 --> 00:37:09.080
And how do I want it to go
differently in the future?

00:37:09.080 --> 00:37:13.152
And I think Business School just really
ingrains that habit in you because, for

00:37:13.152 --> 00:37:17.552
one, a lot of the teachers are advocating
that approach every week, every quarter.

00:37:17.552 --> 00:37:21.280
Some say every day, you're writing
down what you want to happen,

00:37:21.280 --> 00:37:25.130
why you want it to happen, what
are you going to do to make it happen?

00:37:25.130 --> 00:37:29.290
It's like this huge exercise and
self reflection.

00:37:29.290 --> 00:37:30.384
But you do it so

00:37:30.384 --> 00:37:36.180
much that I think it starts to kind
of embed itself as a personal habit.

00:37:36.180 --> 00:37:39.106
And I've seen a lot of benefits from that,

00:37:39.106 --> 00:37:43.740
just being more intentional with
how I spend my time and therefore,

00:37:43.740 --> 00:37:49.043
you see that you achieve more of the
things that you actually want to achieve.

00:37:49.043 --> 00:37:52.518
And you don't spend time on
the stuff that you don't find as.

00:37:52.518 --> 00:37:54.339
&gt;&gt; Awesome Sang.

00:37:54.339 --> 00:37:55.378
How about yourself?

00:37:55.378 --> 00:37:58.630
How did you think about
leadership potential coming in?

00:37:58.630 --> 00:37:59.870
What did you want to develop?

00:37:59.870 --> 00:38:05.890
Or looking back, what was an area that
you found yourself growing a lot in?

00:38:05.890 --> 00:38:09.741
&gt;&gt; Yes, so I think the first
one that comes to mind for

00:38:09.741 --> 00:38:15.153
me is before business school,
I was always in these kind of high stress

00:38:15.153 --> 00:38:20.220
situations and I had effective but
not great coping mechanisms.

00:38:20.220 --> 00:38:23.630
So I would, call a friend and
rant about work or

00:38:23.630 --> 00:38:28.875
I would order a pizza or just I had
all these like systems about how,

00:38:28.875 --> 00:38:33.885
I'm having a hard time at work this
is how I'm going to get through it.

00:38:33.885 --> 00:38:37.907
And those things work when you're 22 or
24.

00:38:37.907 --> 00:38:42.332
But one of the things I took away,
was this idea of peer coaching and

00:38:42.332 --> 00:38:44.554
then ultimately self coaching.

00:38:44.554 --> 00:38:48.665
And so there are classes that
are formally built around this, but

00:38:48.665 --> 00:38:52.714
it's also just embedded in the DNA
of the entire student class.

00:38:52.714 --> 00:38:57.564
Where in every class, formal or
informal you end up reaching out to

00:38:57.564 --> 00:39:02.510
your very talented and smart and
wise classmates for their advice.

00:39:02.510 --> 00:39:07.300
Asking them for how they would approach
a problem, what they would do.

00:39:07.300 --> 00:39:11.290
And then ultimately,
through exercises like journaling,

00:39:11.290 --> 00:39:16.138
like reflection, or there's even
classes where you send out a survey and

00:39:16.138 --> 00:39:21.310
solicit feedback from people about
your own behaviors and your brand.

00:39:21.310 --> 00:39:24.005
But you kind of develop this
way of self coaching and

00:39:24.005 --> 00:39:26.904
I think that's something
that I really appreciate.

00:39:26.904 --> 00:39:31.083
And now that I've been out of GSB for
a while, where now when I feel stressed,

00:39:31.083 --> 00:39:34.105
I'm not like you know door
dashing a pizza all the time,

00:39:34.105 --> 00:39:38.190
I may be just taking a step back and
thinking, okay, why are you stressed?

00:39:38.190 --> 00:39:43.377
What's happening, is this,
what can you do or, go for a walk or

00:39:43.377 --> 00:39:47.910
you kind of learn these processes for
handling things.

00:39:47.910 --> 00:39:53.224
And I think that's something that sounds
silly but, it's really taking very

00:39:53.224 --> 00:39:58.537
smart and hardworking people but showing
them how they can maybe be as smart and

00:39:58.537 --> 00:40:03.140
as hardworking but in a healthier and
more sustainable way.

00:40:03.140 --> 00:40:06.682
You've this idea of self
coaching I find so fascinating.

00:40:06.682 --> 00:40:11.940
Do you have any insights or tips for folks
out there about maybe something that you

00:40:11.940 --> 00:40:17.445
learned along the way that others might be
able to adapt into kind of there everyday?

00:40:19.583 --> 00:40:25.579
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I think one thing is this
act of writing things down and

00:40:25.579 --> 00:40:28.176
journaling to yourself.

00:40:28.176 --> 00:40:33.541
We live in such a fast digital age that
taking pen to paper can seem really silly.

00:40:33.541 --> 00:40:38.123
But [COUGH] kind of like Brian alluded to,
there is a lot of power in sitting down,

00:40:38.123 --> 00:40:41.468
whether that's every day or
every quarter or whatever.

00:40:41.468 --> 00:40:44.633
And writing your own thoughts out and
kind of seeing it on paper and

00:40:44.633 --> 00:40:46.162
even just going through that.

00:40:46.162 --> 00:40:51.727
And it's something that whenever
I can be consistent with it,

00:40:51.727 --> 00:40:54.248
I do notice a lot of benefit.

00:40:54.248 --> 00:40:58.482
And so I think just journaling
self-written reflections,

00:40:58.482 --> 00:41:03.720
goal setting anything like that could be,
I think, it's very powerful.

00:41:03.720 --> 00:41:08.476
&gt;&gt; All right, so we are getting close to
the end and I want to make sure we go to

00:41:08.476 --> 00:41:13.891
a area that I find very near and dear to
my heart, [LAUGH] the application process.

00:41:13.891 --> 00:41:17.899
And so for everyone out there
as awesome as our panelists are,

00:41:17.899 --> 00:41:23.389
as awesome as our students and alumni are,
they have no idea why they were admitted.

00:41:23.389 --> 00:41:27.050
And they probably will never know,
[LAUGH] why they were admitted.

00:41:27.050 --> 00:41:30.496
For all things admissions related
if you're curious about the process

00:41:30.496 --> 00:41:33.082
definitely check in with
the MBA admissions office or

00:41:33.082 --> 00:41:34.241
check out our website.

00:41:34.241 --> 00:41:36.861
There's a ton of great information there.

00:41:36.861 --> 00:41:40.200
But that doesn't mean there
might have been some learnings,

00:41:40.200 --> 00:41:42.971
talking about journaling and
self-reflection.

00:41:42.971 --> 00:41:47.366
Some things kind of on this back end
that you might have wished that you did

00:41:47.366 --> 00:41:50.987
a little bit more or
have might be able to share with people.

00:41:50.987 --> 00:41:54.916
So maybe Shawn would start with you,
and I'm going to be selfish and

00:41:54.916 --> 00:41:59.077
go around the circle he'll Shawn,
Coolio, Cong, and then Brian.

00:41:59.077 --> 00:42:02.422
So maybe go in reverse
chronological order.

00:42:02.422 --> 00:42:08.240
Thinking back to when you were
applying to business school,

00:42:08.240 --> 00:42:13.258
any tips that you would give
a younger you to help make

00:42:13.258 --> 00:42:18.184
that process as kind of
approachable as possible?

00:42:19.825 --> 00:42:20.758
&gt;&gt; Good question.

00:42:20.758 --> 00:42:26.228
It's interesting because my fiance
is actually she's in business school

00:42:26.228 --> 00:42:30.976
at Stanford right now, and I, [INAUDIBLE]
&gt;&gt; Can you repeat that

00:42:30.976 --> 00:42:34.119
please I think you broke out for
just a little bit.

00:42:34.119 --> 00:42:36.136
&gt;&gt; Yeah, of course, sorry, so

00:42:36.136 --> 00:42:40.424
my fiance is actually at Stanford
right now, getting her MBA.

00:42:40.424 --> 00:42:43.452
She's class of 2022,
social starter, second year.

00:42:43.452 --> 00:42:47.364
So I don't really remember my application
process, I certainly remember hers.

00:42:47.364 --> 00:42:51.617
Because one of the more reasons who I was
more stressed out when you're kind of

00:42:51.617 --> 00:42:55.617
watching someone you've apply versus
when you yourself are applying.

00:42:55.617 --> 00:43:01.413
And I think what something she did that I
thought was really helpful was outside and

00:43:01.413 --> 00:43:04.361
was just talking to students in my class.

00:43:04.361 --> 00:43:09.001
She tried to reach out to a lot of alumni
that were relatively recently graduated,

00:43:09.001 --> 00:43:10.826
just to get a sense of the place.

00:43:10.826 --> 00:43:14.075
Because she'd been there obviously as
a significant other when I was a student,

00:43:14.075 --> 00:43:16.337
but it's very different than
the student experience.

00:43:16.337 --> 00:43:22.251
So she was, Hey,

00:43:22.251 --> 00:43:25.517
sorry, I think my headphones died,
hopefully you can hear me?

00:43:25.517 --> 00:43:28.176
It's probably as I need to wrap this up.

00:43:28.176 --> 00:43:32.649
I think was most helpful was talk to as
many students as possible current and

00:43:32.649 --> 00:43:36.767
otherwise recently graduated to just
get a sense of what the place and

00:43:36.767 --> 00:43:37.769
the culture is.

00:43:37.769 --> 00:43:42.274
Because I think reflection of that in your
essay is obviously important, but more so

00:43:42.274 --> 00:43:44.887
understanding that it's the right fit for
you.

00:43:44.887 --> 00:43:48.905
Because I'm always amazed by folks like
will and the rest of the admission class.

00:43:48.905 --> 00:43:52.805
How they're able to stitch together
a group of folks who are so

00:43:52.805 --> 00:43:56.035
different set up so
much in common at the same time.

00:43:56.035 --> 00:44:00.016
So that's the thing that's critical is
understanding what Stanford's about,

00:44:00.016 --> 00:44:02.219
what do you think you're
going to get out of it?

00:44:02.219 --> 00:44:03.955
And what the places like?

00:44:03.955 --> 00:44:05.897
And I think the easiest way to do that,

00:44:05.897 --> 00:44:08.947
is through conversations with
people who've been there.

00:44:08.947 --> 00:44:11.781
&gt;&gt; Yeah, and for
folks who might not know how to start,

00:44:11.781 --> 00:44:15.982
definitely I would also recommend either
checking out some of our webinars.

00:44:15.982 --> 00:44:20.276
because there are times when you just
aren't able to connect at that individual

00:44:20.276 --> 00:44:23.080
level maybe it's time, resources, network.

00:44:23.080 --> 00:44:27.305
The point of many of our summer series is
to provide an opportunity to learn more

00:44:27.305 --> 00:44:30.901
about the student experience from
folks who just went through it.

00:44:30.901 --> 00:44:34.174
We're also recording many of these and
posting them online.

00:44:34.174 --> 00:44:37.866
So as you're thinking about some
of these topics through the year,

00:44:37.866 --> 00:44:42.467
if you're thinking for the future you'll
have a repository of information that you

00:44:42.467 --> 00:44:43.858
can always come back to.

00:44:43.858 --> 00:44:47.191
And we're always here curious to
know what you're interested in and

00:44:47.191 --> 00:44:50.416
that helps me created new events for
you as well so, thanks Shawn.

00:44:50.416 --> 00:44:52.797
Carlile, what about yourself?

00:44:52.797 --> 00:44:54.273
Tips for a younger you.

00:44:54.273 --> 00:44:59.585
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] I think the part
where I won't provide that

00:44:59.585 --> 00:45:06.194
much insight into is the actual
like GMAT you got to crunch and

00:45:06.194 --> 00:45:10.871
spin and that's was the worst part for me.

00:45:10.871 --> 00:45:15.466
I don't know if anyone else on
this panel has good ways for

00:45:15.466 --> 00:45:18.927
how to get through that, please share.

00:45:18.927 --> 00:45:20.951
But for
me that was definitely the hardest part.

00:45:20.951 --> 00:45:23.839
The second hardest part was, okay,

00:45:23.839 --> 00:45:28.230
that's done, I got the score I want,
that's the way.

00:45:28.230 --> 00:45:33.426
It's crafting your story and
I think that's incredibly

00:45:33.426 --> 00:45:38.621
important as you're going to bind to,
say eight schools,

00:45:38.621 --> 00:45:44.481
you have eight schools there,
they all have different essays.

00:45:44.481 --> 00:45:47.303
But ultimately you're
telling the same story, and

00:45:47.303 --> 00:45:51.873
you're branding yourself to tell that
same story across different environments.

00:45:51.873 --> 00:45:58.553
So it's figuring out and spending the time
upfront to figure out what your story is.

00:45:58.553 --> 00:46:03.532
And there's a lot of
self-reflection involved in

00:46:03.532 --> 00:46:07.364
figuring out what matters most to you.

00:46:07.364 --> 00:46:12.072
And then finding strings
that tie those passions,

00:46:12.072 --> 00:46:15.576
to all of your different activities,

00:46:15.576 --> 00:46:21.061
all your different careers
that I promise you it's there.

00:46:21.061 --> 00:46:26.111
And then once you find it you can pull
on personal and professional experiences

00:46:26.111 --> 00:46:30.642
to really tell that holistic story
to the school you're applying to.

00:46:30.642 --> 00:46:32.377
&gt;&gt; Okay, thank you, Cong.

00:46:33.868 --> 00:46:39.045
&gt;&gt; So I think my one advice would be,
I remember feeling so intimidated and

00:46:39.045 --> 00:46:44.843
thinking that there's 10,000 people
that apply and only so many get in.

00:46:44.843 --> 00:46:49.624
And so my advice be think about what you
would contribute to your class in addition

00:46:49.624 --> 00:46:53.083
to just thinking about everything
Stanford has to offer.

00:46:53.083 --> 00:46:56.978
Because the reality is once you get there,
you are part of the DNA.

00:46:56.978 --> 00:47:01.604
And so you will be helping your classmates
and bringing your lived experiences.

00:47:01.604 --> 00:47:05.799
And so I think that is something
I didn't do enough of, I was so

00:47:05.799 --> 00:47:07.911
intimidated and just scared.

00:47:07.911 --> 00:47:12.833
But really think about if you were
a student in this community and

00:47:12.833 --> 00:47:14.026
at this place.

00:47:14.026 --> 00:47:17.747
What are some of the things that only
you could bring to your classmates or

00:47:17.747 --> 00:47:20.133
you could bring to the experience for
others?

00:47:20.133 --> 00:47:24.513
And that I think is a great way to kind of
get your wheels moving both in terms of

00:47:24.513 --> 00:47:27.125
figuring out your own
story like Khail said.

00:47:27.125 --> 00:47:30.978
But also tying your lived experiences and
your dreams and

00:47:30.978 --> 00:47:36.123
aspirations with Stanford and kind of
bringing those two things together.

00:47:36.123 --> 00:47:37.948
&gt;&gt; Then, Brian taking us home.

00:47:40.749 --> 00:47:44.756
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I think I'll just say one thing,
which is,

00:47:44.756 --> 00:47:48.220
what I noticed at school
was shocking to me.

00:47:48.220 --> 00:47:51.896
How many people in my class said
they almost didn't apply to

00:47:51.896 --> 00:47:55.583
Stanford because they thought
that they wouldn't get in.

00:47:55.583 --> 00:48:00.202
So I think my recommendation is,
if you think that the Stanford

00:48:00.202 --> 00:48:04.659
program excites you,
please do yourself a favor and apply.

00:48:08.262 --> 00:48:14.248
&gt;&gt; Thank you, and to that effect,
I encourage you if you haven't done so

00:48:14.248 --> 00:48:19.031
already, continue this
journey at mystanford.mba.

00:48:19.031 --> 00:48:21.794
You're able to do things like
create a personalized dashboard.

00:48:21.794 --> 00:48:26.992
So, if you are in the process of applying,
there's an application tracker so

00:48:26.992 --> 00:48:30.400
you'll be able to know where and
each step you are.

00:48:30.400 --> 00:48:33.224
If you're thinking about
either this year or

00:48:33.224 --> 00:48:37.620
the future we also launched application
week, just this past month.

00:48:37.620 --> 00:48:41.465
And we've saved the recordings
again on our GSB YouTube channel,

00:48:41.465 --> 00:48:44.281
including workshops on
how to start your story,

00:48:44.281 --> 00:48:47.456
ways to communicate your
story in the written format.

00:48:47.456 --> 00:48:51.619
As well as what are different ways of
thinking about how do you communicate it

00:48:51.619 --> 00:48:54.742
verbally, whether that is
thinking about networking or

00:48:54.742 --> 00:48:56.770
even a behavioral based interview?

00:48:56.770 --> 00:48:58.172
How do you prepare for that?

00:48:58.172 --> 00:49:02.312
How are you able to most effectively
communicate who you are?

00:49:02.312 --> 00:49:03.950
What matters to you?

00:49:03.950 --> 00:49:06.054
What you've done and how you've done it?

00:49:06.054 --> 00:49:09.586
So, thank you again,
everyone for joining us today.

00:49:09.586 --> 00:49:11.505
Thank you to our panelists.

00:49:11.505 --> 00:49:15.326
Hopefully, we'll be able to interact
much more again in the future.

00:49:15.326 --> 00:49:17.782
But until then, take care, stay safe and

00:49:17.782 --> 00:49:20.598
have a wonderful rest of
the weekend everyone.

00:49:20.598 --> 00:49:25.560
[MUSIC]

